MONAI basic project with code
A basic MONAI project typically involves three pillars: Transforms (data prep), Networks (model architecture), and Datasets (efficient loading). 

The most common "starter" project is 2D Medical Image Classification using the MedNIST dataset, which contains 58,954 2D images across 6 categories (e.g., AbdomenCT, CXR, ChestCT). 

🛠️ Basic Setup & Code Example
This example demonstrates a minimal pipeline to load data, apply transforms, and initialize a pre-built MONAI model. 

python
import torch
from monai.networks.nets import DenseNet121
from monai.transforms import (
    Compose, LoadImage, EnsureChannelFirst, 
    ScaleIntensity, ToTensor
)
from monai.apps import MedNISTDataset

# 1. Define Pre-processing Transforms
train_transforms = Compose([
    LoadImage(image_only=True),
    EnsureChannelFirst(),
    ScaleIntensity(),
    ToTensor(),
])

# 2. Use MONAI Apps for automatic data handling
# This downloads and extracts the dataset if not present
train_ds = MedNISTDataset(root_dir="./data", section="training", 
                          transform=train_transforms, download=True)
train_loader = torch.utils.data.DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=300, shuffle=True)

# 3. Initialize a MONAI-optimized Network
device = torch.device("cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu")
model = DenseNet121(
    spatial_dims=2,   # 2D images
    in_channels=1,    # Grayscale
    out_channels=6    # 6 classes in MedNIST
).to(device)

# 4. Standard PyTorch Training Setup
loss_function = torch.nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), 1e-5)
Use code with caution.

🔑 Essential Concepts for Beginners
Dictionary-based Transforms: While the code above uses "Array" transforms, MONAI is famous for Dictionary transforms (e.g., LoadImaged) which allow you to apply the same operations to both images and labels simultaneously.
CacheDataset: For medical imaging (like 3D MRI), use CacheDataset instead of a standard PyTorch Dataset to store transformed data in memory, speeding up training by up to 10x.
Sliding Window Inference: For large 3D volumes that don't fit in GPU memory, MONAI provides a sliding_window_inference function to process them in overlapping crops. 

For a more complex 3D project, you can follow the Spleen 3D Segmentation Tutorial on GitHub. 
